iversal, low- and middle-income, or below a set threshold) and no provider grants.\n- **Two passages I set aside:**\n  - In the CBS debate (2026-05-15) he says the state should move \"in the direction of early childhood education\". I coded that `adjacent`: it is about what children learn in care, not about how far public money reaches.\n  - In the KQED interview (2026-03-05) he says his parents couldn't afford childcare. I coded that as `rhetorical` and the quote as not quotable.\n- **Judgment calls:**\n  - I coded the campaign page as `statement-other` rather than `statement-answer`, because the page is about affordability in general, not childcare.\n  - The page has no date, so I gave it the year \"2026\" from the current campaign.\n  - `rests_on` is empty because no chair is seated.\n- **needs_source:** I listed his own words on whether childcare support should be universal or limited by income (a questionnaire or debate answer), and a childcare-specific plan page if one exists.\n\nThe two transcripts that don't mention childcare (Commonwealth Club and the Nexstar debate) are not coded as passages.","ttft_ms":14966,"type":"result","duration_ms":42148,"uuid":"d733a284-19c3-4f4b-a9dd-a4d89957b6b6","ttft_stream_ms":1674,"time_to_request_ms":75,"first_content_frame_ms":1674,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
